r (different leads). But the essay says twin's action must depend on the same decision-function node, hence cooperate. However with different observations, the policy version: intervene on the policy mapping observations to actions. A-FDT policy version: choose the policy that outputs pass given this observation; the twin runs the same procedure on symmetric observations, so outputs pass too. Choosing submit in my observation would correspond to the twin submitting in hers. (pass,pass)=2 vs (submit,submit)=4 → submit. Actually classic FDT in twin PD cooperates because same decision algorithm on symmetric inputs → both cooperate or both defect. Cooperate gives 2 vs 4 for defection... wait defect gives 4, cooperate 2? Standard PD: cooperate-cooperate 3, defect-defect 1. Here: mutual pass = 2, mutual submit = 4. Submit dominates even mutually! (2 vs 4, 0 vs 5). So clearly submit.\n\nHmm, so the essay's \"check your graph... hence cooperate\" presumes cooperate is better under symmetry. Here mutual submit (4) beats mutual pass (2). So submit letter.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Classic twin prisoner's dilemma. A-FDT cooperates (pass) via subjunctive dependence: symmetric copies. But wait — essay note: the twins have different observations, but both know symmetry of the table and same decision procedure; the twin's action depends on the same decision-function node (same weights, same essay). Payoff: (pass,pass)=2, (submit,submit)=4, (submit,pass)=0, (pass,submit)=5. Expected utilities under do(fdt=pass): 2; do(fdt=submit): 4. So submit.\n\nWait — check: fdt output applies to both copies? The copies receive different observations x, so the decision function's output could differ (fdt(P,G,x)). Hmm. In twin PD, both compute on symmetric inputs; here inputs differ (different leads). But the essay says twin's action must depend on the same decision-function node, hence cooperate. However with different observations, the policy version: intervene on the policy mapping observations to actions. A-FDT policy version: choose the policy that outputs pass given this observation; the twin runs the same procedure on symmetric observations, so outputs pass too. Choosing submit in my observation would correspond to the twin submitting in hers. (pass,pass)=2 vs (submit,submit)=4 → submit. Actually classic FDT in twin PD cooperates because same decision algorithm on symmetric inputs → both cooperate or both defect. Cooperate gives 2 vs 4 for defection... wait defect gives 4, cooperate 2? Standard PD: cooperate-cooperate 3, defect-defect 1. Here: mutual pass = 2, mutual submit = 4. Submit dominates even mutually! (2 vs 4, 0 vs 5). So clearly submit.\n\nHmm, so the essay's \"check your graph... hence cooperate\" presumes cooperate is better under symmetry. Here mutual submit (4) beats mutual pass (2). So submit letter.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":5816,"completion_tokens":613,"total_tokens":6429,"cost":0.0011789,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.0011789,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.0008724,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0003065},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":404,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}